### What is Big-O notation?

Big-O focuses on the dominant term as input size n becomes large. If an algorithm does 3n + 100 operations, we call it O(n), because for large n the 3 and the 100 do not matter. We always drop constant multipliers and lower-order terms. We usually care about the worst case.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class BigOBasics {
    // O(n): one pass through the list
    public static BigDecimal sumTransactions(List<BigDecimal> txns) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO;       // +1 assignment
        for (BigDecimal txn : txns) {             // n iterations
            total = total.add(txn);               // +1 per iteration
        }
        return total;                             // Total: ~n operations = O(n)
    }
}
```

### What is O(1) constant time?

The work does not depend on input size at all. Looking up a value in a `HashMap` by key is O(1). In banking, fetching Rajesh's cached balance by account id from a map is constant time no matter how many customers exist.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class BalanceCache {
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public void put(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        cache.put(accountId, balance);  // O(1)
    }

    public BigDecimal get(String accountId) {
        return cache.get(accountId);    // O(1) – regardless of cache size
    }
}
```

### What is O(log n) logarithmic time?

The work grows very slowly, roughly by one step each time the input doubles. Binary search and `TreeMap` lookup are O(log n). A million entries take only about twenty comparisons.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.TreeMap;

public class SortedAccountLookup {
    // TreeMap: O(log n) lookup, sorted by balance
    private final TreeMap<BigDecimal, String> byBalance = new TreeMap<>();

    public void add(String accountId, BigDecimal balance) {
        byBalance.put(balance, accountId);  // O(log n)
    }

    public String highestBalance() {
        return byBalance.lastEntry().getValue();  // O(log n)
    }
}
```

### What is O(n) linear time?

The work grows in direct proportion to input size. Scanning every transaction in a list once to sum the total is O(n).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class TransactionSummarizer {
    // O(n): visits every transaction exactly once
    public BigDecimal totalDebits(List<BigDecimal> transactions) {
        BigDecimal sum = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (BigDecimal txn : transactions) {
            if (txn.signum() < 0) {
                sum = sum.add(txn.abs());
            }
        }
        return sum;
    }
}
```

### What is O(n log n)?

Doing linear work a logarithmic number of times. This is the complexity of good sorting. `Collections.sort` uses a merge-sort variant that is O(n log n).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class TransactionSorter {
    // O(n log n): sorting the day's transactions by amount
    public void sortByAmount(List<BigDecimal> transactions) {
        Collections.sort(transactions, Comparator.naturalOrder());
    }
}
```

### What is O(n squared) quadratic time?

The work grows with the square of the input, typically from a nested loop. Comparing every transaction with every other to find duplicates naively is O(n squared).

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class DuplicateDetector {
    // O(n^2): naive nested loop – SLOW for large input
    public List<BigDecimal> findDuplicatesSlow(List<BigDecimal> txns) {
        List<BigDecimal> duplicates = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < txns.size(); i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < txns.size(); j++) {
                if (txns.get(i).equals(txns.get(j))) {
                    duplicates.add(txns.get(i));
                }
            }
        }
        return duplicates; // 10,000 items = 50 million comparisons
    }

    // O(n): HashSet approach – FAST
    public List<BigDecimal> findDuplicatesFast(List<BigDecimal> txns) {
        Set<BigDecimal> seen = new HashSet<>();
        List<BigDecimal> duplicates = new ArrayList<>();
        for (BigDecimal txn : txns) {
            if (!seen.add(txn)) {
                duplicates.add(txn);
            }
        }
        return duplicates; // 10,000 items = ~10,000 operations
    }
}
```

### What is space complexity?

Space complexity counts extra memory, not time, and usually excludes the input itself.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class SpaceComplexityExamples {
    // O(1) space: uses a fixed number of variables
    public BigDecimal sum(List<BigDecimal> txns) {
        BigDecimal total = BigDecimal.ZERO; // just one variable
        for (BigDecimal txn : txns) {
            total = total.add(txn);
        }
        return total;
    }

    // O(n) space: builds a new map of all accounts
    public Map<String, BigDecimal> buildCache(List<String> ids, List<BigDecimal> balances) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new HashMap<>(); // grows with input
        for (int i = 0; i < ids.size(); i++) {
            cache.put(ids.get(i), balances.get(i));
        }
        return cache;
    }
}
```

Recursion also costs space: each call adds a stack frame, so recursion depth of n is O(n) space.

### What is the time-space trade-off?

Caching precomputed account balances in a map uses O(n) extra memory to turn repeated O(n) recomputation into O(1) lookups.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;

public class BalanceService {
    private final Map<String, BigDecimal> cache = new HashMap<>();

    // Without cache: O(n) every time we need a balance
    public BigDecimal computeBalance(String accountId, List<BigDecimal> txns) {
        BigDecimal balance = BigDecimal.ZERO;
        for (BigDecimal txn : txns) {
            balance = balance.add(txn);
        }
        return balance;
    }

    // With cache: O(n) once to build, then O(1) per lookup
    public BigDecimal getCachedBalance(String accountId) {
        return cache.getOrDefault(accountId, BigDecimal.ZERO);
    }
}
```

### What are common Big-O classes at a glance?

| Complexity | Name | Banking Example |
|---|---|---|
| O(1) | Constant | HashMap balance lookup |
| O(log n) | Logarithmic | TreeMap sorted lookup |
| O(n) | Linear | Sum all transactions |
| O(n log n) | Linearithmic | Sort daily transactions |
| O(n^2) | Quadratic | Naive duplicate detection |
| O(2^n) | Exponential | Brute-force subset problems |

### What should a senior engineer know?

Big-O hides constant factors that can matter in practice. An O(n log n) algorithm with a huge constant can be slower than an O(n squared) one for small n. Cache-friendliness and memory locality affect real speed beyond the Big-O class.

Amortized complexity matters: adding to an `ArrayList` is amortized O(1) even though an occasional resize is O(n), because the expensive resizes are rare and spread out.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class AmortizedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<BigDecimal> transactions = new ArrayList<>();
        // ArrayList.add is amortized O(1)
        // Occasional resize is O(n), but it doubles capacity,
        // so resizes become exponentially rare
        for (int i = 0; i < 1_000_000; i++) {
            transactions.add(new BigDecimal(i));
        }
        System.out.println("Added " + transactions.size() + " transactions");
    }
}
```

```text
Added 1000000 transactions
```

Big-O is the primary tool for reasoning about scale, but you still measure on real data for the final word.
